  #line-at(72pt, 438.768pt, "Abstract:")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 452.204pt, "Black phosphorus is an emerging low-dimensional semiconductor whose tunable band gap, high carrier ")
  #line-at(72pt, 465.64pt, "mobility, and anisotropic transport make it attractive for directional energy transport in electronics and ")
  #line-at(72pt, 479.075pt, "thermoelectrics. Using ultrafast stroboscopic optical scattering microscopy (stroboSCAT), we image ")
  #line-at(72.011pt, 492.511pt, "spatiotemporal evolution of energy carriers with picosecond resolution, simultaneously resolving heat and ")
  #line-at(72.022pt, 505.947pt, "charge transport in bulk black phosphorus and exciton transport in the monolayer. The in-plane ")
  #line-at(72.044pt, 519.382pt, "armchair:zigzag anisotropy increases from 5.3:1 in the bulk to 6.7:1 in the monolayer, driven by enhanced ")
  #line-at(72.044pt, 532.818pt, "armchair exciton diffusivity (45.5 cm²/s) relative to bulk armchair charge carriers (16.5 cm²/s). The ")
  #line-at(72.033pt, 546.143pt, "monolayer anisotropy is smaller than the calculated exciton dispersion alone would suggest, which first-")
  #line-at(72.033pt, 559.579pt, "principles calculations attribute to preferential exciton-phonon scattering to large momenta along the ")
  #line-at(72.022pt, 573.015pt, "zigzag direction. These directly measured, layer-resolved diffusivities identify armchair-aligned monolayers ")
  #line-at(72.022pt, 586.45pt, "as the optimal geometry for directional transport and show that transport anisotropy cannot be inferred ")
  #line-at(72.022pt, 599.886pt, "from band dispersion alone. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.022pt, 626.757pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.022pt, 640.193pt, "Black phosphorus (BP) is a layered elemental semiconductor with a high carrier mobility, tunable bandgap, ")
  #line-at(72.055pt, 653.629pt, "and strongly anisotropic transport properties. BP has reported carrier mobilities on the order of 500-1000 ")
  #line-at(72.044pt, 667.064pt, "cm")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.44pt, 663.24pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(88.92pt, 667.2pt, "/(V*s), exceeding that of transition metal dichalcogenides and approaching that of graphene")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(516pt, 663.24pt, "1,2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(524.64pt, 667.2pt, ". In ")
  #line-at(72pt, 680.525pt, "contrast to graphene, however, BP has a layer-dependent tunable electronic structure, with an optical gap ")
  #line-at(72.022pt, 693.961pt, "varying from 0.3 eV in bulk material to ~1.5 eV in the monolayer, owing to the combined effects of ")
  #line-at(72.022pt, 707.397pt, "quantum confinement and layer-dependent screening")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(323.28pt, 703.44pt, "3–6")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(333.84pt, 707.4pt, ". Additionally, owing to its puckered crystal ")
