  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 82.44pt, "and interpreted, especially in the way that thermal signatures are/not distinguished from electronic ones")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(533.04pt, 78.48pt, "28")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.003pt, 95.876pt, "which we carefully describe in the SI, and the extent to which excitation densities promote higher order ")
  #line-at(72.003pt, 109.311pt, "carrier-carrier interactions. Previous work has established a heat diffusivity on the order of 0.1-0.4 cm")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(528.24pt, 105.36pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(531.72pt, 109.32pt, "/s ")
  #line-at(72.003pt, 122.756pt, "with a ~2:1 anisotropy ratio favoring the zigzag axes that decreases for thinner films")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(457.2pt, 118.8pt, "10,11")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(473.04pt, 122.76pt, ". To within our ")
  #line-at(72.001pt, 136.085pt, "uncertainty, our results are consistent with this previous work.  ")
  #line-at(72.001pt, 162.957pt, "The electronic transport in both the bulk material and the monolayer (charge carriers and excitons, ")
  #line-at(72.001pt, 176.392pt, "respectively) provide a useful comparison in their own right. Here, the transport anisotropy ratio between ")
  #line-at(72.023pt, 189.828pt, "the armchair and zigzag axes changes with layer thickness, going from 5.3:1 in bulk to 6.7:1 in the ")
  #line-at(72.012pt, 203.264pt, "monolayer. The increase in this anisotropy in the monolayer is primarily due to the strong increase in ")
  #line-at(72.012pt, 216.699pt, "armchair direction exciton diffusivity of 45.5 cm")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(289.56pt, 212.76pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(293.04pt, 216.72pt, "/s relative to the ambipolar transport of electrons and ")
  #line-at(71.997pt, 230.156pt, "holes in the bulk of 16.5 cm")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(194.16pt, 226.2pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(197.64pt, 230.16pt, "/s. Naively, one might expect that the thickness dependence decreases at the ")
  #line-at(71.994pt, 243.596pt, "monolayer level by favoring free carriers to bound excitons, or that the scattering time or exciton or defect ")
  #line-at(71.994pt, 257.031pt, "densities could play a role. Although Boltzmann transport calculations for the bulk material are currently ")
  #line-at(71.994pt, 270.357pt, "prohibitive, intuition from the layer dependence of the excitonic/electronic band structure calculations")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(533.16pt, 266.4pt, "38")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.003pt, 283.796pt, "support the observed enhancement: while the dispersion does become shallower as the number of layers ")
  #line-at(72.036pt, 297.231pt, "is decreased to a single one, the range of momenta near the Γ point where this is true becomes smaller. ")
  #line-at(72.047pt, 310.667pt, "This raises the interesting question of how to relate the dispersion in mobility to experimental ")
  #line-at(72.036pt, 324.103pt, "observations, which presumably include carriers with multiple momenta. As we accumulate additional ")
  #line-at(72.036pt, 337.538pt, "combined experimental/computational transport results on additional systems in the future, it should be ")
  #line-at(72.036pt, 350.974pt, "possible to develop more explicit such relationships, although the observed agreement between theory ")
  #line-at(72.036pt, 364.41pt, "and experiment here suggests that the current strategy that we have employed is generally acceptable.    ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 391.281pt, "Beyond the technical and scientific details, this work affirms that monolayer black phosphorus offers the ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 404.606pt, "possibility of high-mobility and directional transport, which could be highly useful for next-generation field ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 418.042pt, "effect transistors. Furthermore, the anisotropy in electronic and thermal transport could be leveraged not ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 431.478pt, "only for thermal management in devices but also for thermoelectric applications in which waste heat can ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 444.913pt, "be converted back to useful work. Diagnostic tools like stroboSCAT, especially in combination with ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 458.349pt, "advanced electronic structure and dynamics calculations, to incisively characterize the potential for such ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 471.785pt, "effects will be important not only for BP but also for a range of other materials in which heat and charge ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 485.22pt, "excitations manifest prominently and need to be spatiotemporally distinguished and resolved. For ")
  #line-at(72.014pt, 498.656pt, "example, other thermoelectric van der Waals materials")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
